// Generated by tools/build_content.py. Part 3: The ER model
export default {"lead": "<p>An entity-relationship (ER) diagram shows what you store data about (entities) and how those things connect (relationships). On the practicum you are graded on three things: the right entities, the right relationships, and the right minimum and maximum cardinality at every line end.</p>", "secs": [{"id": "p3-what-the-er-model-is-for", "t": "What the ER model is for", "h": "<ul><li>It is a <strong>conceptual-level</strong> model, used early in design to capture the mini-world.</li><li>It is a <strong>semantic model</strong>: it captures meaning, not storage.</li><li>It is not tied to any DBMS.</li><li>Non-experts can read it, so it is used to get feedback from users, and later to document the finished database.</li><li>At this stage the goal is \"conceptually correct\", meaning it makes sense to the users. Making it technically correct for relational theory comes later (Parts 4 and 5).</li></ul><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Version</th><th>Origin</th><th>Why you care</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Chen</td><td>Peter Chen, 1976, the original</td><td>The textbook and legacy documentation use it. You must be able to read it.</td></tr><tr><td>IE (Information Engineering), \"crow's foot\"</td><td>James Martin, 1990</td><td>The notation used in class and on the practicum</td></tr><tr><td>IDEF1X</td><td>A national standard from NIST</td><td>Know it exists</td></tr><tr><td>UML</td><td>Object Management Group</td><td>Supports object-oriented design</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"id": "p3-chen-vs-crow-s-foot-side-by-side", "t": "Chen vs crow's foot, side by side", "h": "<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Chen</th><th>IE (crow's foot)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Entity</td><td>Rectangle</td><td>Rectangle, sometimes sectioned</td></tr><tr><td>Attribute</td><td>Oval attached to the entity</td><td>Listed inside the rectangle (optional)</td></tr><tr><td>Identifier</td><td>Underlined attribute</td><td>Placed in the top section of the rectangle</td></tr><tr><td>Relationship</td><td>Diamond on the line</td><td>Just the line</td></tr><tr><td>Multivalued attribute</td><td>Double oval</td><td>Not drawn; you remove it (see patterns below)</td></tr><tr><td>Derived attribute</td><td>Dashed oval</td><td>Not drawn</td></tr><tr><td>Weak entity</td><td>Double rectangle with a double diamond</td><td>An ID-dependent child entity</td></tr><tr><td>Maximum cardinality</td><td>1, M or N written on the line</td><td>Hash mark for one, crow's foot for many</td></tr><tr><td>Minimum cardinality</td><td>Double line for total participation, single line for partial</td><td>Hash mark for mandatory, circle for optional</td></tr><tr><td>Subtypes</td><td>Specialization circle with d (disjoint) or o (overlapping)</td><td>Circle with an X (exclusive) or an empty circle (inclusive)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"id": "p3-entities", "t": "Entities", "h": "<p>The working definition from lecture: <strong>an entity is anything you want to store data about.</strong> It can be a person, place, event, object or concept, physical or abstract.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Entity instance</td><td>One particular thing</td><td>The customer Tammy Bryant</td></tr><tr><td>Entity type</td><td>The category, the permanent definition (the intension)</td><td>CUSTOMER</td></tr><tr><td>Entity set (entity class)</td><td>All instances of the type that exist right now (the extension)</td><td>Every customer currently in the database</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The rectangle on a diagram stands for the whole set, never for a single instance.</p><p>Three tests for \"is this really an entity?\", from the instructor's NSFdb walkthrough:</p><ul><li><strong>There is only one of it.</strong> Then it is not an entity. The company itself and the design project are never entities.</li><li><strong>Only its name would be stored.</strong> Then it is an attribute of something else. Carrier (UPS, FedEx) is an attribute of a shipment unless more data about carriers is needed.</li><li><strong>Someone else manages that data.</strong> Then it is out of scope. The shopping cart is run by a third-party site, so it is not in NSFdb.</li></ul>"}, {"id": "p3-attributes", "t": "Attributes", "h": "<p>An attribute is a defining property of an entity type.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Domain</td><td>The set of allowable values for an attribute</td><td>Credits: whole numbers 0 to 150</td></tr><tr><td>Null</td><td>No value at all. Zero and a blank string are values, not nulls.</td><td>A supplier with no main contact recorded</td></tr><tr><td>Multivalued attribute</td><td>One instance can have several values</td><td>A customer with three email addresses</td></tr><tr><td>Composite attribute</td><td>Can be broken into smaller parts</td><td>Address into street, city, state, zip</td></tr><tr><td>Derived attribute</td><td>Can be calculated instead of stored</td><td>Age from date of birth</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For practicum diagrams you show no attributes on ordinary entities. Attributes mentioned in the problem are clues about structure, not things to draw.</p>"}, {"id": "p3-keys", "t": "Keys", "h": "<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Definition</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Identifier</td><td>The informal ER-stage stand-in for a primary key. It should tell instances apart but does not have to be perfect.</td><td>Student name on an early diagram</td></tr><tr><td>Superkey</td><td>Any attribute or set of attributes that uniquely identifies an instance</td><td>{stuId}, and also {stuId, credits}</td></tr><tr><td>Candidate key</td><td>A minimal superkey: no proper subset of it is also a superkey</td><td>{stuId}; {socSecNo}</td></tr><tr><td>Composite key</td><td>A key made of more than one attribute</td><td>{State, City}</td></tr><tr><td>Primary key</td><td>The candidate key chosen to identify instances</td><td>stuId</td></tr><tr><td>Alternate key</td><td>A candidate key not chosen as the primary key</td><td>socSecNo</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary key</td><td>An attribute used to look records up, not necessarily unique</td><td>lastName</td></tr><tr><td>Foreign key</td><td>A relational-model term, not an ER term: the primary key of one table repeated in another to make a relationship</td><td>Customer_ID in ORDERS</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Uniqueness is a statement about meaning, not about the sample you happen to see. No two students share a last name today, but lastName is still not a superkey. This idea returns as the \"logical test\" in practicum Question 2.</p>"}, {"id": "p3-relationships", "t": "Relationships", "h": "<p>A relationship is a connection between entity sets, taken from the real world: customers place orders, students take classes.</p><p><strong>Degree</strong> is the number of entity sets a relationship links.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Degree</th><th>Name</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Unary, also called recursive</td><td>An employee manages other employees</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Binary (by far the most common)</td><td>A customer places orders</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Ternary</td><td>Course, faculty and textbook together</td></tr><tr><td>n</td><td>N-ary</td><td>Any larger combination</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The practicum review problems tell you not to use ternary relationships, and Question 1 allows only binary or recursive. Stick to those two.</p><p><strong>Relationship names</strong> are optional. Add one only when the meaning is not obvious: when two different relationships connect the same pair of entities, or when the relationship is recursive.</p><p><strong>Has-a vs is-a.</strong> Every relationship above is a has-a relationship: a customer has an order. An is-a relationship says one entity is a special type of another: a graduate student is a student. Is-a relationships are drawn as supertypes and subtypes (see the patterns below).</p>"}, {"id": "p3-maximum-cardinality", "t": "Maximum cardinality", "h": "<p>Maximum cardinality is the largest number of instances of one entity that a single instance of the other entity can be related to. It is defined at both ends of the relationship. \"Many\" means more than one is possible.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>One-to-one (1:1)</td><td>Each X relates to at most one Y, and each Y to at most one X</td><td>A chairperson chairs one department; a department has one chairperson</td></tr><tr><td>One-to-many (1:M)</td><td>Each X can relate to many Y, but each Y relates to at most one X</td><td>A customer places many orders; an order belongs to one customer</td></tr><tr><td>Many-to-many (M:N)</td><td>Each X can relate to many Y, and each Y to many X</td><td>A student takes many classes; a class has many students</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}, {"id": "p3-minimum-cardinality-participation", "t": "Minimum cardinality (participation)", "h": "<p>Minimum cardinality is the smallest number of instances that an instance must be related to. In practice the only question is zero or one.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Minimum</th><th>Called</th><th>Textbook term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>Optional</td><td>Partial participation</td><td>An instance may exist with no related instance</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Mandatory</td><td>Total participation</td><td>Every instance must have at least one related instance</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>NSFdb example: a customer can exist with zero orders (optional), but an order must belong to a customer (mandatory).</p>"}, {"id": "p3-reading-crow-s-foot-notation", "t": "Reading crow's foot notation", "h": "<p>Each end of a relationship line carries two symbols. The symbol touching the entity is the <strong>maximum</strong> (hash mark = one, crow's foot = many). The symbol just outside it is the <strong>minimum</strong> (hash mark = one, circle = zero).</p><figure class=\"fig\"><div class=\"scroll\"><svg class=\"dia\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 312\" role=\"img\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMin meet\" aria-label=\"Each line end carries a maximum and a minimum\" font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"24\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Each line end carries a maximum and a minimum</text><g><path d=\"M40 86H92\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"114\" y1=\"79\" x2=\"114\" y2=\"93\"></line><line x1=\"108\" y1=\"79\" x2=\"108\" y2=\"93\"></line><path d=\"M120 86L92 86\"></path></g><rect x=\"120\" y=\"68\" width=\"60\" height=\"36\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"150\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">B</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">One and only one</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"146\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">min 1, max 1</text></g><g><path d=\"M220 86H272\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"294\" y1=\"79\" x2=\"294\" y2=\"93\"></line><circle cx=\"284\" cy=\"86\" r=\"5\"></circle><path d=\"M300 86L289 86M279 86L272 86\"></path></g><rect x=\"300\" y=\"68\" width=\"60\" height=\"36\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"330\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">B</text><text x=\"290\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Zero or one</text><text x=\"290\" y=\"146\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">min 0, max 1</text></g><g><path d=\"M400 86H452\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M480 79L467 86L480 93\"></path><line x1=\"461\" y1=\"79\" x2=\"461\" y2=\"93\"></line><path d=\"M480 86L452 86\"></path></g><rect x=\"480\" y=\"68\" width=\"60\" height=\"36\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"510\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">B</text><text x=\"470\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">One or many</text><text x=\"470\" y=\"146\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">min 1, max many</text></g><g><path d=\"M580 86H632\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M660 79L647 86L660 93\"></path><circle cx=\"640\" cy=\"86\" r=\"5\"></circle><path d=\"M660 86L645 86M635 86L632 86\"></path></g><rect x=\"660\" y=\"68\" width=\"60\" height=\"36\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"690\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">B</text><text x=\"650\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Zero or many</text><text x=\"650\" y=\"146\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">min 0, max many</text></g><text x=\"24\" y=\"186\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">Reading a line: stand on one entity, then read the symbols at the far end</text><g><rect x=\"110\" y=\"202\" width=\"140\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><rect x=\"510\" y=\"202\" width=\"140\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"256\" y1=\"229\" x2=\"256\" y2=\"215\"></line><line x1=\"262\" y1=\"229\" x2=\"262\" y2=\"215\"></line><path d=\"M250 222L278 222\"></path></g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M510 215L497 222L510 229\"></path><circle cx=\"490\" cy=\"222\" r=\"5\"></circle><path d=\"M510 222L495 222M485 222L482 222\"></path></g><path d=\"M278 222H482\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><text x=\"180\" y=\"226\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">CUSTOMERS</text><text x=\"580\" y=\"226\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">ORDERS</text></g><g><path d=\"M264 238V256\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><text x=\"264\" y=\"274\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">Each order has</text><text x=\"264\" y=\"291\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">one and only one customer</text></g><g><path d=\"M496 238V256\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><text x=\"496\" y=\"274\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">Each customer has</text><text x=\"496\" y=\"291\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">zero or many orders</text></g></svg></div><figcaption>crow&#x27;s foot notation key: four end symbols and one worked reading</figcaption></figure><p>The rule that prevents most lost points: <strong>the symbols next to an entity say how many of that entity one instance of the other entity can have.</strong> To place the symbols at the ORDERS end, stand on one customer and ask \"how many orders?\" To place the symbols at the CUSTOMERS end, stand on one order and ask \"how many customers?\"</p><p>The four end symbols, each drawn at the B end of a line from A:</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Symbol at the B end</th><th>Min</th><th>Max</th><th>Read as</th><th>Phrases in a problem that mean this</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"crow\" data-a=\"A\" data-ae=\"\" data-be=\"1,1\" data-b=\"B\"></span></td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>One and only one</td><td>\"exactly one\", \"one and only one\", \"must be related to one\"</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"crow\" data-a=\"A\" data-ae=\"\" data-be=\"0,1\" data-b=\"B\"></span></td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>Zero or one</td><td>\"at most one\", \"may have one\", \"zero to one\"</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"crow\" data-a=\"A\" data-ae=\"\" data-be=\"1,M\" data-b=\"B\"></span></td><td>1</td><td>Many</td><td>One or many</td><td>\"one or more\", \"at least one\", \"one to many\", \"must have\"</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"crow\" data-a=\"A\" data-ae=\"\" data-be=\"0,M\" data-b=\"B\"></span></td><td>0</td><td>Many</td><td>Zero or many</td><td>\"zero to many\", \"any number\", \"may have many\", \"none or several\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Warning about the textbook: its (min,max) notation in Figure 3.10 is written next to the entity it describes, which is the opposite end from where crow's foot puts the same information. Do not copy positions from one notation into the other.</p>"}, {"id": "p3-the-design-patterns-the-practicum-is-built-from", "t": "The design patterns the practicum is built from", "h": "<p>Every full ERD problem in the past practicums is assembled from the same handful of structures. Learn to spot each one from the wording.</p><p><strong>1. Parent and child (plain one-to-many).</strong> The parent is on the \"one\" side and the child on the \"many\" side. Wording: \"each region has one to many zones; each zone belongs to one and only one region.\"</p><p><strong>2. Many-to-many, and the association entity.</strong> When a many-to-many relationship has data that belongs to the relationship itself, and not to either entity alone, replace the relationship with a new entity that sits between the two. The instructor calls it an association entity, associative entity or association pattern. The textbook's version is a \"descriptive attribute of a relationship\", such as grade on Enroll.</p><ul><li>How to spot it: the problem mentions something to store that needs both entities to be known. Quantity needs the order and the item. Date added to a waitlist needs the student and the section. Loan balance per co-signer needs the customer and the loan.</li><li>How to draw it: each original entity becomes the \"one and only one\" side of a one-to-many relationship to the new entity. The minimum and maximum cardinalities from the original many-to-many move to the association entity's ends.</li><li>If the many-to-many relationship has no data of its own, leave it as a plain many-to-many line on the ER diagram. It still becomes its own table later (Part 4).</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><div class=\"scroll\"><svg class=\"dia\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 282\" role=\"img\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMin meet\" aria-label=\"Data about the pairing turns a many-to-many line into an entity\" font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"24\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Data about the pairing turns a many-to-many line into an entity</text><text x=\"24\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">Before: many-to-many</text><g><rect x=\"60\" y=\"74\" width=\"140\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><rect x=\"560\" y=\"74\" width=\"140\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M200 101L213 94L200 87\"></path><circle cx=\"220\" cy=\"94\" r=\"5\"></circle><path d=\"M200 94L215 94M225 94L228 94\"></path></g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M560 87L547 94L560 101\"></path><circle cx=\"540\" cy=\"94\" r=\"5\"></circle><path d=\"M560 94L545 94M535 94L532 94\"></path></g><path d=\"M228 94H532\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><text x=\"130\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">SPECIES</text><text x=\"630\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">DIVE</text><text x=\"380\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">depth and environment of each sighting: where do they go?</text></g><text x=\"24\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">After: an association entity between them</text><g><rect x=\"60\" y=\"180\" width=\"140\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><rect x=\"310\" y=\"180\" width=\"140\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"var(--accent)\" fill-opacity=\"0.12\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"></rect><rect x=\"560\" y=\"180\" width=\"140\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"206\" y1=\"207\" x2=\"206\" y2=\"193\"></line><line x1=\"212\" y1=\"207\" x2=\"212\" y2=\"193\"></line><path d=\"M200 200L228 200\"></path></g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M310 193L297 200L310 207\"></path><circle cx=\"290\" cy=\"200\" r=\"5\"></circle><path d=\"M310 200L295 200M285 200L282 200\"></path></g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M450 207L463 200L450 193\"></path><circle cx=\"470\" cy=\"200\" r=\"5\"></circle><path d=\"M450 200L465 200M475 200L478 200\"></path></g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"554\" y1=\"193\" x2=\"554\" y2=\"207\"></line><line x1=\"548\" y1=\"193\" x2=\"548\" y2=\"207\"></line><path d=\"M560 200L532 200\"></path></g><path d=\"M228 200H282\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><path d=\"M478 200H532\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><text x=\"130\" y=\"204\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">SPECIES</text><text x=\"380\" y=\"204\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">SIGHTING</text><text x=\"630\" y=\"204\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">DIVE</text><text x=\"380\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">holds depth, dive-time, environment</text><text x=\"380\" y=\"258\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">each sighting has one and only one species and one and only one dive</text></g></svg></div><figcaption>association entity: before and after, from the Reef practicum</figcaption></figure><p><strong>3. The line-item pattern</strong> (the transaction processing model). A header record with many detail lines, each line pointing to a catalog item: CUSTOMER, ORDER, ORDER LINE ITEM, ITEM. The line item entity is really an association entity between the header and the item, carrying data such as quantity. Wording that signals it: \"line items\", \"statement\", \"paycheck\", \"schedule\", \"parts list\", \"each order contains one or more items\". If you recognize the pattern, draw the line-item entity straight away.</p><p><strong>4. Recursive relationships.</strong> An entity related to itself. The test: are the \"other things\" the same kind of thing? Co-workers of infected patients are also infected patients, so there is one entity, not two.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><div class=\"scroll\"><svg class=\"dia\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 232\" role=\"img\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMin meet\" aria-label=\"A recursive relationship is one entity with a line back to itself\" font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"24\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">A recursive relationship is one entity with a line back to itself</text><g><rect x=\"40\" y=\"70\" width=\"120\" height=\"44\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"166\" y1=\"99\" x2=\"166\" y2=\"85\"></line><circle cx=\"176\" cy=\"92\" r=\"5\"></circle><path d=\"M160 92L171 92M181 92L188 92\"></path></g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"93\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"107\" y2=\"120\"></line><circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"130\" r=\"5\"></circle><path d=\"M100 114L100 125M100 135L100 142\"></path></g><path d=\"M188 92H212V166H100V142\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><text x=\"100\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">MEMBER</text><text x=\"126\" y=\"196\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">One-to-one</text><text x=\"126\" y=\"213\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">roommate, or spouse</text></g><g><rect x=\"290\" y=\"70\" width=\"120\" height=\"44\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M410 99L423 92L410 85\"></path><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"92\" r=\"5\"></circle><path d=\"M410 92L425 92M435 92L438 92\"></path></g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"343\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"357\" y2=\"120\"></line><circle cx=\"350\" cy=\"130\" r=\"5\"></circle><path d=\"M350 114L350 125M350 135L350 142\"></path></g><path d=\"M438 92H462V166H350V142\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><text x=\"350\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">FACULTY</text><text x=\"416\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">supervised</text><text x=\"362\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">supervisor</text><text x=\"376\" y=\"196\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">One-to-many</text><text x=\"376\" y=\"213\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">immediate supervisor</text></g><g><rect x=\"540\" y=\"70\" width=\"120\" height=\"44\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M660 99L673 92L660 85\"></path><circle cx=\"680\" cy=\"92\" r=\"5\"></circle><path d=\"M660 92L675 92M685 92L688 92\"></path></g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M593 114L600 127L607 114\"></path><circle cx=\"600\" cy=\"134\" r=\"5\"></circle><path d=\"M600 114L600 129M600 139L600 142\"></path></g><path d=\"M688 92H712V166H600V142\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><text x=\"600\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">PATIENT</text><text x=\"626\" y=\"196\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Many-to-many</text><text x=\"626\" y=\"213\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">co-workers</text></g></svg></div><figcaption>recursive relationships: one-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-many</figcaption></figure><ul><li>A recursive many-to-many that carries its own data becomes an association entity with two relationships back to the same entity (employee family relationships with a type and start and end dates).</li><li>If a maximum is a specific number, write the number beside the crow's foot (an elephant has at most 2 parents).</li></ul><p><strong>5. Supertypes and subtypes (is-a).</strong> Use them when different types of an entity share most attributes but each type also has data stored only for it. Wording: \"share some common attributes but differ on others\", \"each category has some unique data\".</p><ul><li>Without subtypes you would put every type's special attributes in one entity and leave them empty for the other types. Those are <strong>type-specific nulls</strong> and should be avoided.</li><li>The <strong>supertype</strong> holds the attributes common to all. Each <strong>subtype</strong> holds only the attributes unique to it, plus the same identifier as the supertype.</li><li>Do not create subtypes when every type stores the same data. A subtype is also justified if it takes part in a relationship the whole supertype does not (only undergraduates tutor; only administrators review pay records).</li><li><strong>Exclusive</strong> (X in the circle): an instance can be in only one subtype. <strong>Inclusive</strong> (empty circle): an instance can be in more than one.</li><li>A <strong>subtype discriminator</strong> is the supertype attribute that tells which subtype an instance belongs to (isGradStudent).</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><div class=\"scroll\"><svg class=\"dia\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 290\" role=\"img\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMin meet\" aria-label=\"The circle says whether an instance can be in more than one subtype\" font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"24\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">The circle says whether an instance can be in more than one subtype</text><g><rect x=\"130\" y=\"60\" width=\"140\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><path d=\"M200 100V119M200 141V160M110 180V160H290V180\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"130\" r=\"11\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"></circle><path d=\"M192 122L208 138M208 122L192 138\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"></path><rect x=\"50\" y=\"180\" width=\"120\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><rect x=\"230\" y=\"180\" width=\"120\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"200\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">STUDENT</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"204\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">UNDERGRAD</text><text x=\"290\" y=\"204\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">GRAD</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"248\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Exclusive: X in the circle</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">a student is undergraduate or graduate, never both</text></g><g><rect x=\"490\" y=\"60\" width=\"140\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><path d=\"M560 100V119M560 141V160M470 180V160H650V180\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><circle cx=\"560\" cy=\"130\" r=\"11\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"></circle><rect x=\"410\" y=\"180\" width=\"120\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><rect x=\"590\" y=\"180\" width=\"120\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"560\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">EMPLOYEE</text><text x=\"470\" y=\"204\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">PLAYER</text><text x=\"650\" y=\"204\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">COACH</text><text x=\"560\" y=\"248\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">Inclusive: empty circle</text><text x=\"560\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">an employee can be a player and a coach</text></g></svg></div><figcaption>supertype and subtypes: exclusive vs inclusive, the two lecture examples</figcaption></figure><p>A double line between the supertype and the circle means full specialization: every supertype instance must belong to a subtype.</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Class term</th><th>Textbook (EE-R) term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Supertype / subtype</td><td>Superset / subset</td><td>The general entity and its special types</td></tr><tr><td>Is-a relationship</td><td>Specialization (top-down) or generalization (bottom-up)</td><td>The link between them</td></tr><tr><td>Exclusive</td><td>Disjoint (d)</td><td>One subtype at most</td></tr><tr><td>Inclusive</td><td>Overlapping (o)</td><td>More than one subtype allowed</td></tr><tr><td>Full specialization</td><td>Total specialization (double line)</td><td>Every supertype instance must be in some subtype</td></tr><tr><td>Partial specialization</td><td>Partial specialization (single line)</td><td>Some supertype instances are in no subtype</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The textbook also covers predicate-defined and attribute-defined specialization (membership decided by a condition, such as credits under 30 means freshman), shared subsets with multiple inheritance (a teaching assistant is both a graduate student and faculty), and unions or categories (a sponsor is a team or a department or a club).</p><p><strong>6. Multivalued attributes become child entities.</strong> \"Owners may have many phones and many addresses\" means PHONE and ADDRESS are their own entities, each a child of OWNER. The practicum direction \"do not use any structures that imply multi-valued attributes would exist\" is checking for this.</p><p><strong>7. Weak and ID-dependent entities.</strong> Entity Y is <strong>existence dependent</strong> on X if every Y must have a corresponding X. If Y also has no candidate key of its own, Y is a <strong>weak entity</strong> and X is its <strong>strong entity</strong>. A weak entity may have a <strong>partial key</strong> (discriminator) that tells apart the weak instances belonging to the same strong instance.</p><ul><li>Example: APARTMENT 201 means nothing without its BUILDING. The apartment's identifier is the building identifier plus the apartment number, so APARTMENT is weak and ID-dependent.</li><li>Not every existence-dependent entity is weak. An order cannot exist without a customer, but it has its own Order_ID.</li><li>This matters for deletion rules: with ON DELETE CASCADE, deleting a building deletes its apartments.</li><li>Past practicums state that ID-dependent vs non-ID-dependent does not have to be shown and is not graded.</li></ul><p><strong>8. Two relationships between the same pair of entities.</strong> Legal and common. A nurse staffs stations (many-to-many) and a nurse administrates a station (one-to-one). Name both lines.</p><p><strong>9. History.</strong> \"It is important to know what list was in effect historically\" or \"multiple years of data\" means one parent has many dated child records instead of a single current one.</p>"}, {"id": "p3-the-instructor-s-five-step-method", "t": "The instructor's five-step method", "h": "<ol><li><strong>Identify entities.</strong> What do we want to store data about? Read the narrative and mark candidates; discard the ones that fail the three tests above.</li><li><strong>Identify relationships between entities</strong>, both has-a and is-a, with at least the maximum cardinalities.</li><li><strong>Draw the initial diagram</strong>, with at least maximum cardinalities, entity names only.</li><li><strong>Inspect every many-to-many relationship.</strong> Is there data to store about the relationship? If yes, turn it into an association entity.</li><li><strong>Add minimum cardinalities and validate.</strong> Minimums affect little else, so leave them to the end. Validating means rereading the requirements and confirming the design does everything they ask.</li></ol><p>Worked on the NSFdb requirements narrative:</p><div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Sentence in the narrative</th><th>What it becomes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>\"All customers in this database have orders. Customers can have many orders.\"</td><td>CUSTOMERS to ORDERS, one-to-many. A customer must have at least one order; an order has one and only one customer.</td></tr><tr><td>\"Each order contains one or more purchased items... the quantity ordered must be stored for each item on each order.\"</td><td>Line-item pattern. ORDERS to ORDERLINEITEM, one-to-many, at least one line per order.</td></tr><tr><td>\"Each item in the catalog is in zero to many OrderLineItem records.\"</td><td>ITEMS to ORDERLINEITEM, one-to-many, optional on the line-item end.</td></tr><tr><td>\"Each item ordered is shipped by one or more suppliers. Suppliers can have zero to many order lines... carrier, tracking number and shipping date must be stored.\"</td><td>Many-to-many between ORDERLINEITEM and SUPPLIERS with its own data, so it becomes the association entity SHIPMENTS.</td></tr><tr><td>\"Suppliers may be either distributors or manufacturers... either but not both... no more types.\"</td><td>Is-a: SUPPLIERS supertype with DISTRIBUTORS and MANUFACTURERS subtypes, exclusive, full specialization.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><div class=\"scroll\"><svg class=\"dia\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 376\" role=\"img\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMin meet\" aria-label=\"SHIPMENTS resolves the many-to-many between order lines and suppliers\" font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"24\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">SHIPMENTS resolves the many-to-many between order lines and suppliers</text><g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"176\" y1=\"97\" x2=\"176\" y2=\"83\"></line><line x1=\"182\" y1=\"97\" x2=\"182\" y2=\"83\"></line><path d=\"M170 90L198 90\"></path></g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M280 83L267 90L280 97\"></path><line x1=\"261\" y1=\"83\" x2=\"261\" y2=\"97\"></line><path d=\"M280 90L252 90\"></path></g><path d=\"M198 90H252\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"426\" y1=\"97\" x2=\"426\" y2=\"83\"></line><line x1=\"432\" y1=\"97\" x2=\"432\" y2=\"83\"></line><path d=\"M420 90L448 90\"></path></g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M540 83L527 90L540 97\"></path><line x1=\"521\" y1=\"83\" x2=\"521\" y2=\"97\"></line><path d=\"M540 90L512 90\"></path></g><path d=\"M448 90H512\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"176\" y1=\"217\" x2=\"176\" y2=\"203\"></line><line x1=\"182\" y1=\"217\" x2=\"182\" y2=\"203\"></line><path d=\"M170 210L198 210\"></path></g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M280 203L267 210L280 217\"></path><circle cx=\"260\" cy=\"210\" r=\"5\"></circle><path d=\"M280 210L265 210M255 210L252 210\"></path></g><path d=\"M198 210H252\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M357 190L350 177L343 190\"></path><line x1=\"357\" y1=\"171\" x2=\"343\" y2=\"171\"></line><path d=\"M350 190L350 162\"></path></g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"573\" y1=\"116\" x2=\"587\" y2=\"116\"></line><line x1=\"573\" y1=\"122\" x2=\"587\" y2=\"122\"></line><path d=\"M580 110L580 138\"></path></g><path d=\"M350 162V150H580V138\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M653 110L660 123L667 110\"></path><circle cx=\"660\" cy=\"130\" r=\"5\"></circle><path d=\"M660 110L660 125M660 135L660 138\"></path></g><g stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"667\" y1=\"184\" x2=\"653\" y2=\"184\"></line><line x1=\"667\" y1=\"178\" x2=\"653\" y2=\"178\"></line><path d=\"M660 190L660 162\"></path></g><path d=\"M660 138V162\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path></g><g><rect x=\"30\" y=\"70\" width=\"140\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"100\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">CUSTOMERS</text></g><g><rect x=\"280\" y=\"70\" width=\"140\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"350\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">ORDERS</text></g><g><rect x=\"540\" y=\"70\" width=\"160\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"620\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">ORDERLINEITEM</text></g><g><rect x=\"30\" y=\"190\" width=\"140\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"100\" y=\"214\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">SUPPLIERS</text></g><g><rect x=\"280\" y=\"190\" width=\"140\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"var(--accent)\" fill-opacity=\"0.12\" stroke=\"var(--accent)\" stroke-width=\"2\"></rect><text x=\"350\" y=\"214\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">SHIPMENTS</text><text x=\"350\" y=\"250\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">association entity: carrier,</text><text x=\"350\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">tracking number, ship date</text></g><g><rect x=\"560\" y=\"190\" width=\"140\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"630\" y=\"214\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">ITEMS</text></g><g><path d=\"M97 230V261M103 230V261M100 283V300M94 318V300H275V318\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"272\" r=\"11\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></circle><path d=\"M92 264L108 280M108 264L92 280\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"></path><rect x=\"24\" y=\"318\" width=\"140\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><rect x=\"200\" y=\"318\" width=\"150\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"none\" fill-opacity=\"1\" stroke=\"var(--line-strong)\" stroke-width=\"1.25\"></rect><text x=\"94\" y=\"342\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">DISTRIBUTORS</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"342\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"var(--fg)\">MANUFACTURERS</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"276\" font-size=\"11.5\" fill=\"var(--muted)\">exclusive, full specialization</text></g></svg></div><figcaption>NSFdb final solution: 8 entities, as designed from the requirements narrative</figcaption></figure><p>The class example version of NSFdb relaxes the mandatory children: customers may have zero orders, orders zero items, and so on. The instructor's advice is to make a child mandatory only when the logic demands it and leaving it optional would cause integrity problems, because mandatory children complicate inserting data. On the practicum, follow the problem statement exactly: changing a cardinality changes which requirements are met.</p>"}]};
